# Backend TypeScript Standards

## Error Handling

Always handle errors properly and provide context:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Swallowing errors
try {
  await performOperation()
} catch (e) {
  // Silent failure
}

// ❌ BAD - Generic errors
throw new Error('Failed')

// ✅ GOOD - Proper error handling with context
try {
  await performOperation()
} catch (err) {
  logger.error('Failed to perform operation', { error: err, context: additionalInfo })
  throw new VideoProcessingError('Unable to process video', { cause: err })
}
```

## Async/Await Patterns

Use async/await consistently and avoid callback hell:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Callback style
function getVideo(id: number, callback: (err: Error, video: Video) => void) {
  VideoModel.findByPk(id, (err, video) => {
    callback(err, video)
  })
}

// ✅ GOOD - Async/await
async function getVideo(id: number): Promise<Video> {
  const video = await VideoModel.findByPk(id)
  if (!video) throw new VideoNotFoundError(`Video ${id} not found`)
  return video
}
```

## Sequelize Model Usage

Always use proper type safety with Sequelize models:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Using raw queries without types
const results = await sequelize.query('SELECT * FROM videos')

// ✅ GOOD - Using typed models
const videos = await VideoModel.findAll({
  where: { isLive: true },
  include: [
    { model: VideoChannelModel, required: true },
    { model: VideoFileModel }
  ]
})
```

## Logger Usage

Use the Winston logger with proper context:

```typescript
import { logger } from '@server/helpers/logger'

// ❌ BAD - Console logging
console.log('Video uploaded')

// ✅ GOOD - Structured logging
logger.info('Video uploaded successfully', {
  videoId: video.id,
  userId: user.id,
  size: video.size
})
```

## Transaction Handling

Always use transactions for operations that modify multiple tables:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Using transactions
const video = await sequelizeTypescript.transaction(async t => {
  const videoCreated = await VideoModel.create(videoData, { transaction: t })
  
  await VideoFileModel.create({
    videoId: videoCreated.id,
    ...fileData
  }, { transaction: t })
  
  await TagModel.findOrCreateTags(tags, t)
  
  return videoCreated
})
```

## Type Imports

Import types properly to avoid circular dependencies:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Direct import can cause circular deps
import { MVideoFullLight } from '@server/types/models'

// ✅ GOOD - Use type-only imports when possible
import type { MVideoFullLight } from '@server/types/models'
```

## Validation

Use express-validator for request validation:

```typescript
import { body, param, query } from 'express-validator'
import { isIdValid } from '@server/helpers/custom-validators/misc'

// ✅ GOOD - Comprehensive validation
const videoUpdateValidator = [
  param('id').custom(isIdValid),
  body('name').optional().trim().isLength({ min: 3, max: 120 }),
  body('category').optional().isInt(),
  body('privacy').optional().isInt(),
  areValidationErrors
]
```

## Job Queue Patterns

When creating job queue handlers, follow this structure:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Proper job handler structure
import { Job } from 'bull'
import { logger } from '@server/helpers/logger'

export interface VideoTranscodingPayload {
  videoId: number
  resolution: number
}

async function processVideoTranscoding(job: Job<VideoTranscodingPayload>) {
  const payload = job.data
  
  logger.info('Processing video transcoding', { payload })
  
  try {
    const video = await VideoModel.loadAndPopulateAccountAndServerAndTags(payload.videoId)
    if (!video) {
      throw new Error(`Video ${payload.videoId} not found`)
    }
    
    await transcodeVideo(video, payload.resolution)
    
    logger.info('Video transcoding completed', { videoId: video.id })
  } catch (err) {
    logger.error('Video transcoding failed', { error: err, payload })
    throw err
  }
}
```
